75
00:03:28,820 --> 00:03:30,520
What is the cost for investigation?

76
00:03:32,140 --> 00:03:36,940
This meteor has an antenna from which it is broadcasting a radio transmission.

77
00:03:37,320 --> 00:03:39,000
Okay, that is indeed very weird.

78
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:42,440
A single signal, lasting about...

79
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:44,020
Let me see here.

80
00:03:45,780 --> 00:03:47,480
0.8066 seconds.

81
00:03:49,580 --> 00:03:51,380
Dude, these voices are hilarious.

82
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:54,400
Then it pauses for just as long and repeats again.

83
00:03:56,580 --> 00:03:57,180
Hmm.

84
00:03:57,400 --> 00:03:59,620
The antenna was not added after it was recovered?

85
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:01,020
Nope.

86
00:04:02,780 --> 00:04:05,340
Geoseismic stations detected the meteor's crash landing.

87
00:04:06,380 --> 00:04:10,200
Then a naval boat immediately picked up the unusual signal on its radio...

88
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:12,720
...before the meteor was ever recovered.

89
00:04:14,240 --> 00:04:17,380
This is without a doubt the work of an extraterrestrial life-form.

90
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:20,800
I mean, let's not jump to conclusions here.

91
00:04:22,460 --> 00:04:23,880
An advanced one at that.

92
00:04:24,180 --> 00:04:24,380
Alright.

93
00:04:26,260 --> 00:04:30,940
The signal transmits for 0.8066 seconds, then pauses, then resumes.

94
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:33,160
Your hypothesis?

95
00:04:34,940 --> 00:04:36,520
It's waiting for a response.

96
00:04:38,360 --> 00:04:38,880
Really?

97
00:04:39,740 --> 00:04:41,240
What if it's just like...

98
00:04:41,820 --> 00:04:43,440
...at the stress beacon or something?

99
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:45,100
It was repeating, right?

100
00:04:46,900 --> 00:04:47,680
The signal.

101
00:04:47,780 --> 00:04:48,840
What frequency are we looking at?

102
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,620
It's roughly 1.42 million kilohertz.

103
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,800
Then we're lucky that a world-class astronomer like me is here.

104
00:04:57,900 --> 00:04:58,820
Dr. Akers?

105
00:04:59,020 --> 00:05:00,040
This means something to you?

106
00:05:00,940 --> 00:05:02,300
It's the hydrogen line.

107
00:05:03,260 --> 00:05:04,100
Oh, yeah.

108
00:05:05,700 --> 00:05:06,580
Oh, yeah.

109
00:05:07,320 --> 00:05:08,300
1420 megahertz.

110
00:05:09,100 --> 00:05:10,780
I didn't even register that.

111
00:05:11,780 --> 00:05:12,200
True.

112
00:05:14,240 --> 00:05:16,560
Hydrogen atoms, when their electron changes spin...

113
00:05:16,560 --> 00:05:18,600
...they release electromagnetic radiation.

114
00:05:18,840 --> 00:05:18,960
Yeah.

115
00:05:19,580 --> 00:05:20,500
Hyperfine transition.

116
00:05:21,880 --> 00:05:24,180
And it's always at exactly that frequency.

117
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:27,580
1,420,405 kilohertz.

118
00:05:28,380 --> 00:05:30,000
That's beyond my expertise.

119
00:05:30,960 --> 00:05:33,500
Put simply, what is significant about that frequency?

120
00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:36,640
Well...

121
00:05:36,640 --> 00:05:38,040
...where to start?

122
00:05:39,800 --> 00:05:42,800
Well, I guess the main reason why it's so significant is...

123
00:05:44,260 --> 00:05:45,760
...it's a tracer of hydrogen.

124
00:05:46,680 --> 00:05:47,120
Hydrogen, okay?

125
00:05:48,140 --> 00:05:52,680
And hydrogen is by far the most common element in the universe.

126
00:05:52,920 --> 00:05:53,800
Like, it is everywhere.

127
00:05:54,180 --> 00:05:57,380
Pretty much everything you see up there in the night sky when you see anything...

128
00:05:57,380 --> 00:05:59,480
...is pretty much hydrogen, right?

129
00:05:59,780 --> 00:06:02,700
So having a reliable way of figuring out where it is...

130
00:06:02,700 --> 00:06:03,780
...being able to see it...

131
00:06:03,780 --> 00:06:06,240
...and measure the motion of it and other things...

132
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:07,320
...is kind of a big deal.

133
00:06:09,660 --> 00:06:11,820
So, I know it as the 21 centimeter line, right?

134
00:06:12,500 --> 00:06:13,840
Because that's what you get if you convert it.

135
00:06:14,700 --> 00:06:19,060
But the point is, 21 centimeters is kind of a long wavelength, you know?

136
00:06:19,240 --> 00:06:20,440
Like, it's the size of a human hand.

137
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:23,440
So, it's radio radiation.

138
00:06:23,940 --> 00:06:28,580
Which means it's going to penetrate dust and stuff in the galaxy and everywhere else, you know?

139
00:06:28,980 --> 00:06:33,100
So we can actually probe deeper into clouds of dust...

140
00:06:33,100 --> 00:06:35,300
...and we can see through all this obscuring shit...

141
00:06:35,300 --> 00:06:39,140
...that's in between us and the other side of the galaxy, for example, right?

142
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:43,420
And if you, you know, look at 21 centimeters of other galaxies out there...

143
00:06:43,420 --> 00:06:45,300
...you can, you know, infer the motions of it.

144
00:06:45,640 --> 00:06:46,980
You can make rotation curves.

145
00:06:47,080 --> 00:06:49,820
It's actually what allowed us to first predict the existence of dark matter.

146
00:06:50,980 --> 00:06:52,380
Oh, and by the way, fun fact.

147
00:06:52,720 --> 00:06:55,400
The transition itself is actually extremely rare.

148
00:06:55,940 --> 00:06:59,020
The chance for this even to happen is like...

149
00:06:59,020 --> 00:07:02,960
...I think it happens once every, like, 30, 50 million years, you know?

150
00:07:03,680 --> 00:07:08,000
So, it requires a tiny miracle for this spin flip to even take place.

151
00:07:08,340 --> 00:07:10,620
And then you may say, okay, but how is that even useful then?

152
00:07:11,020 --> 00:07:13,380
There's just so much freaking hydrogen out there, dude!

153
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:17,980
There's so much hydrogen that even with this low probability of this transition happening...

154
00:07:18,740 --> 00:07:23,380
If you look at the sky in 21 centimeters, it lights up like a freaking Christmas tree!

155
00:07:24,120 --> 00:07:25,220
I love it!

156
00:07:25,640 --> 00:07:25,980
Let's go.

157
00:07:26,780 --> 00:07:28,020
It's a universal constant.

158
